      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}=-8\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2} & =-8 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})+3({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}) & =-8 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)+3({x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}+4-4) & =-8 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-2+3({x}_{2}+2)^{2}-12 & =-8 \\\\ 2({x}_{1}-1)^{2}+3({x}_{2}+2)^{2} & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}-1, \\\\  \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{2}^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
